    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c29f48e10628c0cc45ee5f130be108ea4911dad6bc302ffde222abd6909d1528",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "In the classical Euler-Maclaurin representation, increasing remainder order enlarges the validity half-plane, but direct application to the q-summand cannot do so because every derivative retains q^{x(s-1)}. Expanding the periodic Bernoulli factor into Fourier modes and substituting u=q^x converts the blocked remainder into uniformly controlled incomplete-beta modes; integration by parts then shifts the beta parameters so its order genuinely moves the boundary, and the q-to-1 limit reconstructs the classical Euler-Maclaurin corrections and remainder.",
